{"item_id": "atlas_test_00063", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a coffeemaker imported from Mexico", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8521.90.0000):\nThe product in question is a coffeemaker, specifically classified under HTS subheading 8516.71.0020, which pertains to \"Other electrothermic appliances, Coffeemakers, Automatic drip and pump type.\" The ruling (HQ 557331) establishes that the molded plastic components of the coffeemaker, produced from U.S. origin materials, undergo a substantial transformation in Mexico, thus qualifying them as constituent materials for the purpose of the Generalized System of Preferences (GSP). The ruling confirms that the assembly processes performed in Mexico, while not exceedingly complex, are significant enough to meet the double substantial transformation requirement necessary for GSP eligibility. Additionally, the ruling clarifies that the \"shelter fee\" included in the transaction price is part of the appraised value of the imported merchandise, affirming that the transaction value method of appraisement is appropriate. Therefore, the HTS US Code 8521.90.0000 is correctly applied for the imported coffeemaker.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8516.71.00.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8516 covers other electrothermic appliances of a kind used for domestic purposes; subheading 8516.71 names coffee or tea makers", "GRI 6: 8516.71.00 > coffee makers > 8516.71.00.20 automatic drip and pump type (assumed standard electric drip coffeemaker)"], "rationale": "An electric coffeemaker is an electrothermic domestic appliance named in subheading 8516.71 (coffee or tea makers). Assuming the common automatic drip design, the statistical suffix is 8516.71.00.20, consistent with in-force ruling H88216 on electric automatic drip coffeemakers. Country of origin (Mexico) does not change the classification.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8516.71.00.40", "reason": "percolator type; description does not indicate a percolator"}, {"code": "8516.71.00.60", "reason": "other coffee makers; the typical coffeemaker is automatic drip"}, {"code": "7323.93", "reason": "non-electric stovetop coffee makers of base metal; the coffeemaker is assumed electric"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00064", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a woman's 100% nylon lace, knit dress with a 100% polyester knit slip liner", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 6114.90.1030):\nThe product in question is a woman's dress made of 100% nylon lace, which is classified under the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTS) as a knitted garment made of synthetic fibers. The specific HTS subheading for the dress is 6104.43.2010, which covers women's dresses, knitted, of synthetic fibers, categorized as \"other.\" The slip liner, made of 100% polyester, is classified under HTS subheading 6108.11.0010, which pertains to women's slips and similar articles, knitted, of man-made fibers. The duty rates for these classifications are 16.8% ad valorem for the dress and 15.9% ad valorem for the slip liner. Both items are categorized under textile category designations 636 and 652, respectively, and are not subject to quota restraints but require a visa for importation from Macau.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6104.43.20.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 6104 covers women's dresses, knitted or crocheted; chapter 61 note 1 (made up knitted articles) satisfied; the lace is a knit lace", "Section XI subheading note / GRI 3(b) practice: the outer nylon lace shell gives the dress its character; the polyester slip liner is disregarded for fiber classification", "GRI 6: 6104.43 of synthetic fibers > 6104.43.20 other (under 23% wool) > 6104.43.20.10 women's"], "rationale": "The garment is a woman's knitted dress, provided for in heading 6104. The outer shell of 100% nylon (synthetic) knit lace controls the fiber subheading 6104.43, and the attached polyester slip liner does not change the classification as both are synthetic. With no wool, it falls in 6104.43.20 and the women's suffix .10, consistent with N020188 (women's nylon/spandex knit dress in 6104.43.2010).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6204.43", "reason": "the dress is knitted, not woven"}, {"code": "6108.11", "reason": "the polyester liner is an integral lining, not a separately classifiable slip"}, {"code": "6104.43.20.20", "reason": "garment is women's, not girls'"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00065", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "plastic covers for an electric motor controller", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8504.90.9690):\nThe product in question consists of two components: the Base Cover (Item #198-3247) and the Keypad (Item #198-3248), which are both outer components of an electric motor controller. The Base Cover is made from flame retardant plastic resin and is specifically molded to protect the printed circuit board assembly (PCBA) within the motor controller, which is essential for preventing electric shock and protecting electrical circuits. The Keypad is made from silicon rubber and serves as a protective cover for the control switches, allowing user interaction without direct exposure to the electrical components. \n\nBoth components are classified under HTSUS subheading 8504.90.9690, which pertains to parts of electrical transformers, static converters, and inductors, specifically categorized as \"Other parts: Other.\" This classification is appropriate because the Base Cover and Keypad do not function as standalone electrical devices but rather as protective parts integral to the operation of the electric motor controller. The ruling confirms that these items fall under this specific HTS code, which carries a general duty rate of free.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8538.90.60.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8537 covers boards, panels and other bases equipped with apparatus for electric control; a motor controller falls there", "Section XVI note 2(b): the covers are not goods of a specific heading of chapter 84/85 and are suitable solely or principally for use with the controller, so they go to heading 8538 (parts of 8535-8537); chapter 39 note 2 excludes articles of Section XVI", "GRI 6: 8538.90 other > 8538.90.60.00 molded parts"], "rationale": "Plastic covers designed for an electric motor controller are parts without their own heading, so Section XVI note 2(b) sends them to the parts heading of the host apparatus. Treating the controller as electric control apparatus of heading 8537, the covers fall in 8538, and as molded parts in 8538.90.60.00. N339672 classified a molded plastic enclosure for a motor-control PCBA in 8538.90.6000.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3926.90.99", "reason": "chapter 39 note 2 excludes articles of Section XVI; dedicated machine parts go to the parts heading"}, {"code": "8504.90.95", "reason": "would apply only if the controller were a static converter (speed drive) of 8504.40; a motor controller is assumed to be control apparatus of 8537"}, {"code": "8538.90.81", "reason": "the covers are molded parts, which have their own line 8538.90.60"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00067", "reference_source": "ruling:J80890", "product": "an infants sweater and hat set made of 100% cotton knit fabric", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling J80890, gold code 6111.20.6020:\nls are returned to China where they are assembled by looping and linking. The garments are finally shipped from Hong Kong/China directly to USA. ISSUE: What are the classification and country of origin of the subject merchandise? CLASSIFICATION: The applicable tariff provision for Style No. RC819600 and Style No. BC819602, will be 6111.20.6020, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States Annotated (HTSUSA), which provides for babies’ garments and clothing accessories, knitted or crocheted, of cotton, other, other, sets. The general rate of duty will be 8.2% ad valorem. COUNTRY OF ORIGIN - LAW AND ANALYSIS: On December 8, 1994, the President signed into law the Uruguay Round Agreements Act. Section 334 of that Act (codified at 19 U.S.C. 3592) provides new rules of origin for textiles and apparel entered, or withdrawn from warehouse, for consumption, on and after July 1, 1996. On September 5, 1995, Customs published Section 102.21, Customs Regulations, in the Federal Register, implementing Section 334 (60 FR 46188). Thus, effective July 1, 1996, the country of origin of a textile or apparel product shall be determined by sequential application of the general rules set forth in paragraphs (c)(1) through (5) of Section 102.21. Paragraph (c)(1) states that \"The country of origin of a textile or apparel product is the single country, territory, or insular possession in which the good was wholly obtained or produced.\" As the subject merchandise is not wholly obtained or produced in a single country, territory or insular possession, paragraph (c)(1) of Section 102.21 is inapplicable. Paragraph (c)(2) states: \"Where the country of origin of a textile or apparel product cannot be determined under paragraph (c)(1) of this section, the country of origin of the good is the single country, territory, or insular possession in which each of the foreign materials incorporated in that good underwent an applicable change in tariff classification, and/or met any other requirement specified for the good in paragraph (e) of this section:\" Paragraph (e) states that \"The following rules shall apply for purposes of determining the country of origin of a textile or apparel product under paragraph (c)(2) of this section\": HTSUS Tariff shift and/or other requirements 6101-6117 If the good is knit to shape, except for goods of subheading 6117.10 provided for in paragraph (e) (2) of this section, a change to heading 6101 through 6117 from any heading outside that group, provided that the knit-to-shape components are knit in a single country, territory or insular possession. The subject merchandise is classifiable as babies’ knit sets in heading 6111, HTSUS. Section 102.21 (e) requires a tariff shift to headings 6101 through 6117 from any heading outside that group. In this case, a tariff shift to heading 6111, HTSUS, occurs in Canada from components that are classifiable from a heading outside that group. Consequentially, Section 102.21 (c)(2), the tariff shift is applicable. However, Style No. RC819600 and Style No. BC819602 do not qualify for preferential treatment under the NAFTA because one or more of the non-originating materials used in the production of this garment will not undergo the change in tariff classification required by General Note 12(t)/61.35, HTSUSA. Specifically, the style is not “both cut (or knit to shape) and sewn or otherwise assembled in the territory of one or more of the NAFTA parties,” from originating materials as is required by the", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6111.20.60.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the sweater is prima facie a babies' garment of heading 6111 (chapter 61 note 6); the hat alone would be headgear of heading 6505", "GRI 3(b): the sweater and hat are put up together as a retail set; the sweater, by bulk, value and role, gives the set its essential character, so the set goes to heading 6111", "GRI 6: 6111.20 of cotton; the sweater line 6111.20.30 excludes goods imported as parts of sets, so the set falls in 6111.20.60 other > 6111.20.60.20 sets"], "rationale": "The sweater and hat together form a GRI 3(b) retail set, and the knit cotton sweater supplies its essential character, placing it in heading 6111 as babies' garments of cotton. Because the specific sweater line 6111.20.30 excludes articles imported as parts of sets, the set falls to 6111.20.60 and the statistical line for sets, .20, as in G83400 (baby creeper with bib classified as sets). N265147 confirms infant hats alone would go to 6505 and that GRI 3(b) applies to infant gift sets.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6111.20.30.00", "reason": "sweaters line excludes those imported as parts of sets"}, {"code": "6505.00.15.15", "reason": "the hat does not give the set its essential character"}, {"code": "6111.20.60.30", "reason": "used for components entered as parts of sets; the set here is classified as a unit"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00068", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "propylene glycol", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 2914.50.5000):\nThe HTS US Code 2914.50.5000 is applicable to propylene glycol, which is classified under subheading 2905.32.0000 as indicated in the ruling HQ 231039. The ruling confirms that propylene glycol (C.A.S. 57-55-6) is a colorless, odorless, and tasteless liquid primarily used as a chemical intermediate in various applications, including de-icing solutions, polyester production, and as solvents for paints and plastics. The ruling establishes that both imported and domestic propylene glycol are commercially interchangeable based on several criteria, including tariff classification, consistent product nomenclature (Material Number 499202), and minimal price differences (3% higher for export). The classification under HTSUS 2905.32.0000 is supported by the fact that both imported and exported propylene glycol share the same tariff classification, thus validating the use of this HTS US Code.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "2905.32.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: chapter 29 note 1(a) covers separate chemically defined organic compounds; heading 2905 covers acyclic alcohols", "GRI 6: 2905.3 diols > 2905.32 propylene glycol (propane-1,2-diol) > 2905.32.00.00"], "rationale": "Propylene glycol is a separate chemically defined acyclic diol, which chapter 29 note 1 places in chapter 29. Subheading 2905.32 names it expressly (propane-1,2-diol), and the only statistical line is 2905.32.00.00. No precedent ruling on the pure compound was found in the search, but the eo nomine subheading settles the classification.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3824.99", "reason": "applies only to mixtures or preparations, not the pure compound"}, {"code": "2909.49", "reason": "covers ether-alcohols such as dipropylene or polyethylene glycols, not propane-1,2-diol"}, {"code": "3907.29", "reason": "polypropylene glycol (a polyether) is a different product"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00070", "reference_source": "ruling:N096605", "product": "clay cutting and modeling tools", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N096605, gold code 8205.59.5560:\ns in clay. Oven Bake Clay Template (Item # ACLTMP) is a flexible, rectangular clear plastic sheet. The sheet contains several circular die cuts in the middle, ranging in diameter from 1/8th of an inch (3mm) to 1 1/8 inches (28mm). There are ruler markings on two sides of the template. The ruler markings on the top of the template show measurement increments in centimeters, while the markings on the right side of the template show measurement increments in inches. The template is designed for use by those practicing the art of pottery, to measure quantities of clay and products made from clay. The applicable subheading for the Texture Makers (Item #ST1440) will be 3926.90.9980, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for other articles of plastics, other. The rate of duty will be 5.3% ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the Leaf Décor Beginner Project Guide (Item #ST1101) will be 4901.10.0040, HTSUS, which provides for printed books, brochures, leaflets and similar printed matter, whether or not in single sheets: In single sheets, whether or not folded; Other. The rate of duty will be Free. The applicable subheading for the Essential Tool Kit (Item #ASESSKIT) will be 8205.59.5560, HTSUS, which provides for handtools (including glass cutters) not elsewhere specified or included; blow torches and similar self-contained torches; vises, clamps and the like, other than accessories for and parts of machine tools; anvils; portable forges; hand- or pedal-operated grinding wheels with frameworks; base metal parts thereof: other handtools (including glass cutters) and parts thereof: other: other: of iron or steel: other: other (including parts). The rate of duty will be 5.3% ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the Oven Bake Clay Template (Item # ACLTMP) will be 9017.80.0000, HTSUS, which provides for \"Other\" instruments for measuring length for use in the hand. The rate of duty will be 5.3% ad valorem. You suggested classifying the Texture Makers (Item #ST1440) in subheading 3924.90.5600, HTSUS. That classification is incorrect because this item is not of the class or kind of merchandise that is principally used as household, hygienic or toilet articles. You suggested classifying the Essential Tool Kit (Item #ASESSKIT) in subheading 8205.59.5510, HTSUS. The set was classified pursuant to General Rule of Interpretation (GRI) 3(c) and the items that occur last in the tariff are found within subheading 8205.59.5560, HTSUS. You suggest classification for the Oven Bake Clay Template in subheading 9017.20.8080, HTSUS, which covers other drawing, marking-out, or mathematical calculating instruments. Based on the information you provided, the template is not used for drawing or marking out, but rather to measure the length of clay and clay products. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on World Wide Web at http://www.usitc.gov/tata/hts/. Your inquiry does not provide enough information for us to give a classification ruling on The Eight Piece Pottery Tool Set (Item #A8PS). Your request for a classification ruling should include the following: Provide a cost breakdown for each item in the set. What type of metal is present in the handle and the point of the Needle Tool? If more than one metal is present, what metal predominates by weight? What is the material composition of the Loop Tool? Moreover, wh", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8205.59.55.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8205 covers handtools not elsewhere specified or included; clay cutters, loop and ribbon tools are not named in a more specific chapter 82 heading", "GRI 3(b) (if put up as a set): the steel edged cutting tools give the essential character", "GRI 6: 8205.59 other handtools > 8205.59.55 other, of iron or steel > edged handtools > 8205.59.55.10 other"], "rationale": "Clay cutting and modeling tools are handtools not elsewhere specified in heading 8205. Assuming steel working edges, they fall in 8205.59.55 as other handtools of iron or steel and, being edged, in statistical line .10. In-force ruling M83095 classified a comparable pottery tool set (wire clay cutter, loop and ribbon tools, modeling tools) in 8205.59.5510.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8205.59.55.60", "reason": "the clay cutting tools have cutting edges, so the edged handtools line applies"}, {"code": "4417.00", "reason": "would apply only to tools with working parts of wood"}, {"code": "3926.90", "reason": "would apply only to tools wholly of plastic; chapter 82 needs a base metal working part"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00072", "reference_source": "ruling:N341089", "product": "solenoid valve parts used in an auto braking system", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N341089, gold code 8481.90.9040:\nat, “a subpart of a particular part of an article is more specifically provided for as a part of the part than a part of the whole.” Although the end covers are incorporated into a stabilizer bar, they are more specifically parts of valves. As a result, classification of the end covers in subheading 8708.80, HTSUS, is precluded. The end covers, referred to as valve blocks, complete a composite machine, in which the valves complete the primary function, Note 3 to Section XVI noted. Accordingly, each aluminum end cover will be classified as a part within heading 8481, which provides for valves. The applicable subheading for the aluminum manifold end covers (part numbers 22334215, 38024602, and 38070948) will be 8481.90.9040, HTSUS, which provides for Taps, cocks, valves and similar appliances, for pipes, boiler shells, tanks, vats or the like, including pressure-reducing valves and thermostatically controlled valves: Parts: Other: Of valves of subheading 8481.20: Other. The rate of duty will be Free. Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheading 8481.80.90, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to an additional 25 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.01, in addition to subheading 8481.80.9040, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Note cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china, respectively. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Sandra Martinez at sandra.martinez@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8481.90.90.85", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Section XVII note 2(e): articles of heading 8481 (and by extension their dedicated parts) are not 'parts of vehicles', which excludes heading 8708", "Section XVI note 2(b): parts suitable solely or principally for solenoid valves of heading 8481 are classified with those valves, in 8481.90", "GRI 6: 8481.90 parts > 8481.90.90 other (not of hand-operated or check valves) > other > 8481.90.90.85 other"], "rationale": "Solenoid valves are articles of heading 8481, and Section XVII note 2(e) keeps them and their dedicated parts out of heading 8708 even when used in automotive braking systems. Under Section XVI note 2(b), valve parts go to 8481.90, and as parts of solenoid (not hand-operated or check) valves that are not described as valve bodies or steel forgings they fall in 8481.90.90.85. N361135 shows that a valve stem went to 8481.90.9085 while an electromagnet housing went to 8505.90, so the exact part could change the line.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8708.30.50", "reason": "Section XVII note 2(e) excludes articles of 8481 and their parts from vehicle parts"}, {"code": "8505.90.75", "reason": "would apply to solenoid housings/coil parts of the electromagnet itself (as in N361135), not to valve components"}, {"code": "8481.90.90.60", "reason": "valve bodies line; the parts are not stated to be valve bodies"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00076", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "polyester/epoxy powder coatings", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 3907.30.0000):\nThe product in question is a powder coating composed of 45 to 55 percent by weight of polyester or epoxy resin (or binder), along with curing agents, fillers (or extenders), pigments, and additives. The classification of this product is determined by the General Rules of Interpretation (GRI) of the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTS). \n\nInitially, there was a dispute regarding whether the product should be classified under subheading 3207.10.0000 as a prepared pigment or under subheading 3907.30.0000/3907.99.0050 as a resin. However, upon analysis, it was determined that the product does not fit the definition of prepared pigments as outlined in the Explanatory Notes (EN) for Heading 3207, which specifically pertains to inorganic materials used in the ceramic industry.\n\nInstead, the powder coatings are primarily composed of synthetic polymers (polyester or epoxy) and meet the definition of \"primary form\" as per Heading 3907. The EN for Chapter 39 indicates that powder paints consisting principally of plastics and containing additives and pigments, used for application by heat, are classifiable in this chapter. The ruling also clarifies that if the resin composition is solely polyester, it falls under subheading 3907.99.0050, while if it is solely epoxy, it is classified under 3907.30.0000. In cases where there is a combination of both, classification is based on the predominant resin by weight.\n\nTherefore, the HTS US Code for polyester/epoxy powder coatings is 3907.30.0000 when the resin composition is solely epoxy, and it is appropriate to classify these products under Chapter 39, HTSUSA.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3907.99.50.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: powder coatings are not paints or varnishes of 3208-3210 (not dispersed or dissolved in a medium); as polymer powders they are primary forms of chapter 39 (note 6(b))", "Chapter 39 note 4 / subheading note: a blend of polymers is classified according to the polymer predominating by weight; polyester is assumed to predominate", "GRI 6: 3907.9 other polyesters > 3907.99 other (saturated) > 3907.99.50 other > 3907.99.50.50 other"], "rationale": "Powder coatings are polymer powders in primary form rather than paints dispersed in a medium, so they fall in chapter 39. Under chapter 39 note 4 a polymer blend goes to the heading of the polymer predominating by weight; hybrid powders are usually polyester-rich, giving other saturated polyesters, 3907.99.50.50 (H88731 placed a saturated polyester resin for powder coatings in 3907.99; the code it cites, 3907.99.0050, is now line 3907.99.50.50). If epoxy predominates, N239953 supports 3907.30.00.00, so the answer abstains on the resin ratio.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3907.30.00.00", "reason": "epoxide resins; correct only if the epoxy portion predominates by weight"}, {"code": "3208.20", "reason": "paints of heading 3208 must be dispersed or dissolved in a non-aqueous medium; powder coatings are not"}, {"code": "3907.91", "reason": "unsaturated polyesters; hybrid powder polyester is saturated"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00079", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "forged connecting rods used in 14.8L diesel engines for Class 8 heavy trucks", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8409.99.9110):\nThe forged connecting rods in question are specifically designed for use in 14.8L diesel engines, which are typically found in Class 8 heavy trucks. The classification falls under HTSUS subheading 8409.99.9110, which covers \"Parts suitable for use solely or principally with the engines of 8407 or 8408: Other: Other: Other: For vehicles of subheading 8701.20, or heading 8702, 8703 or 8704: Connecting rods.\" The manufacturing process involves cutting round steel bars from South Korea, forging them in Mexico, and subsequently machining them for final assembly into engines, which aligns with the definition of parts for engines. The general duty rate for this classification is 2.5% ad valorem, confirming its correct categorization under the specified HTS code.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8409.99.91.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Section XVI note 2(b): a connecting rod is not a good of its own chapter 84/85 heading (not a transmission shaft of 8483 as it is an integral engine part per Section XVII note 2(e) logic), and it is suitable solely or principally for diesel engines of 8408, so it goes to heading 8409", "Section XVII note 2(e): parts of engines of 8408 are excluded from vehicle parts of 8708", "GRI 6: 8409.99 parts for compression-ignition engines > other (not rough cast iron) > 8409.99.91 to be installed in vehicles of 8701.21-.29, 8702, 8703 or 8704 > 8409.99.91.10 connecting rods"], "rationale": "Forged connecting rods for a 14.8L diesel engine are parts suitable solely or principally for compression-ignition engines of heading 8408, so Section XVI note 2(b) places them in 8409, and Section XVII note 2(e) excludes them from 8708. Because the engines go into Class 8 heavy trucks (8701.2x/8704), subheading 8409.99.91 applies, with the statistical line .10 for connecting rods. N249760 placed piston pins for diesel engines in the same 8409.99.91 subheading.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8409.99.10", "reason": "rough cast-iron parts only; these are forged, not cast"}, {"code": "8483.10", "reason": "connecting rods are engine-specific parts; Section XVI note 2(b) sends dedicated parts of 8408 engines to 8409"}, {"code": "8409.99.99", "reason": "the engines are for on-road trucks of 8701.2x/8704, which have their own line 8409.99.91"}, {"code": "8409.91", "reason": "spark-ignition engine parts; these are for diesel engines"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00080", "reference_source": "ruling:G84446", "product": "a youth combo snorkel pack that includes swim goggles, a snorkel set, a mesh sport bag, and a molded plastic beach capsule for valuables", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling G84446, gold code 9506.29.0040:\nle underwater, and are necessary when engaged in the water sport of snorkeling. The swim goggles are not water sport equipment but rather are used for the protection of the eyes while swimming, usually in a pool and not at the beach where snorkel equipment is usually employed. The storage equipment bag that is constructed of equal amounts of PVC plastic sheeting and man-made textile fiber mesh is not of the kind normally sold with swimming equipment. The molded plastic beach capsule is used while swimming at the beach to hold your valuables that you do not wish to leave behind while you swim. The applicable subheading for the diving mask and snorkel set will be 9506.29.0040, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Water skis, surf boards, sailboards and other water sport equipment; parts and accessories thereof: Other…Other.” The rate of duty will be free. The applicable subheading for the swim goggles will be 9004.90.0000, Harmonized Tariff schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for spectacles, goggles and the like, corrective, protective or other; other. The rate of duty will be 2.5 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the molded plastic storage case for valuables will be 3926.90.9880, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United states (HTS), which provides for “Other articles of plastics and other materials of headings 3901 to 3914 (con.): Other…Other.” The rate of duty will be 5.3 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the vinyl and man-made mesh sport bag with a drawstring closure will be 4202.92.4500, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for other, travel, sport and similar bags, with outer surface of sheeting of plastic or of textile materials, other, other. The duty rate of duty will be 20 percent ad valorem. Please note that there are no visa or quota requirements on this merchandise. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Tom McKenna at 212-637-7015. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski Director, National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9506.29.00.80", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: components fall in different headings: goggles 9004.90, snorkel set 9506.29, mesh bag 4202.92, plastic capsule 3926.90/4202", "GRI 3(b): the items are put up together for retail sale to meet the particular need of snorkeling/swimming; the snorkel set gives the essential character by role and value", "GRI 6: 9506.29.00 water-sport equipment > 9506.29.00.80 other"], "rationale": "The combo pack is a GRI 3(b) retail set of goods of different headings put up together for snorkeling. The snorkel set is the principal article and gives the set its essential character, so the whole pack goes to 9506.29 as other water-sport equipment, statistical line .80. In-force ruling L81175 classified a children's swim combo (mask, snorkel, fins in a case) in 9506.29.0080.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9004.90.00.90", "reason": "the goggles do not give the set its essential character"}, {"code": "4202.92", "reason": "the mesh bag is a container/packing accessory to the set, not its essential character"}, {"code": "9506.29.00.20", "reason": "water skis line; not applicable"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00083", "reference_source": "ruling:N335129", "product": "industrial robots imported without their programmed controllers", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N335129, gold code 8479.50.0000:\n for developers, integrators, and end-users, the system can be programmed by the user to carry out various customized applications and payloads. While the robot is designed primarily for moving material, you provide several examples of other uses including manufacturing, warehousing, clean technology, and laboratories. As imported the B1 robot is not fitted with any handling equipment such as manipulator arms or other types of tooling but is capable of working with such items. As imported the robot includes a dedicated charging station with docking port, power cable, and wired charging cable. The applicable subheading for the Hyundai B1 Robot will be 8479.50.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States, which provides for “Machines and mechanical appliances having individual functions, not specified or included elsewhere in this chapter; parts thereof: Industrial robots, not elsewhere specified or included”. The rate of duty will be 2.5 percent ad valorem. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Arthur Purcell at arthur.purcell@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8479.50.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 2(a)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8479 covers machines having individual functions not specified elsewhere; subheading 8479.50 names industrial robots n.e.s.i.", "GRI 2(a): the robot body without its controller is an incomplete article that, as presented, has the essential character of the complete industrial robot (the manipulator performs the robot's function; the controller is added after import)", "Section XVI note 2 not needed: the article is classified as the incomplete machine, not as a part", "GRI 6: 8479.50.00.00"], "rationale": "Industrial robots imported without their programmed controllers are incomplete machines; under GRI 2(a) the manipulator arm supplies the essential character of the finished robot. With no dedication to welding or material handling shown, they fall under the residual eo nomine provision 8479.50.00.00 for industrial robots n.e.s.i. HQ 962659 treated industrial robots imported incomplete (without end-of-arm tooling) under GRI 2(a) and upheld classification of non-dedicated robots in 8479.50.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8479.90.94", "reason": "the manipulator is not a mere part; under GRI 2(a) it has the essential character of the complete robot"}, {"code": "8428.70", "reason": "industrial robots for lifting/handling would apply only if dedicated to material handling, which is not stated"}, {"code": "8515.21 / 8515.31", "reason": "welding robots require dedication to welding, not shown for robots without controllers or tooling"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00085", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "orthodontic instruments", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9022.13.0000):\nThe HTS US Code 9022.13.0000 is applicable to orthodontic instruments as determined by the classification ruling HQ 559147. The ruling outlines the manufacturing processes for orthodontic instruments, which involve the shaping of rough forgings imported from Germany, processing in Mexico, and subsequent operations in the United States. The final product, which includes the shaped forgings and additional components such as screws from the U.S., is classified under the HTSUS subheading 9018.49.80, which pertains to dental hand instruments. The ruling confirms that the country of origin for these instruments is Mexico, as the essential character of the finished product is imparted by the shaped forgings from Mexico. The classification is consistent with the definitions and requirements outlined in the NAFTA Marking Rules and the relevant Customs Regulations, specifically 19 CFR 102.11, which governs the determination of country of origin for goods. Thus, the HTS US Code 9022.13.0000 accurately reflects the classification of orthodontic instruments.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9018.49.80.40", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9018 covers instruments used in dental sciences; Section XV note 1 and chapter 82 do not cover chapter 90 instruments, so heading 8203 pliers is excluded for instruments specialised for dental use", "GRI 6: 9018.4 dental instruments > 9018.49 other (not drills/burs) > 9018.49.80 other > 9018.49.80.40 dental hand instruments"], "rationale": "Orthodontic instruments are instruments used in dental sciences, named by heading 9018 and excluded from the general tool headings of chapter 82. Assuming they are hand-held pliers, cutters and similar tools, they fall in 9018.49.80 and the statistical line for dental hand instruments, .40. I85144 confirms that orthodontic articles are classified in 9018.49.80 (it used the residual .80 line for non-hand-instrument mixing trays).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8203.20", "reason": "general pliers; orthodontic pliers are specialised dental instruments of chapter 90"}, {"code": "9018.49.80.80", "reason": "residual 'other' line; orthodontic hand instruments fit the dental hand instruments line"}, {"code": "9018.49.40.00", "reason": "dental burs only"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00088", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "an electrical deicer used on aircraft to prevent ice build-up", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8516.80.4000):\nThe product in question is an electrical deicer specifically designed for use on aircraft to prevent ice accumulation. It consists of a composite insulating material that houses a metal electric heating element, which is crucial for its function. The heating elements are enclosed within the composite material and are connected to various wires and sensors, indicating that they are part of an electrical assembly. The applicable HTS subheading, 8516.80.4000, is specifically for electrical heating resistors that are assembled with simple insulated former and electrical connections, which aligns with the product's design and purpose for anti-icing and deicing applications. The ruling confirms this classification, and the duty rate for this HTS code is 3.9 percent ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8516.80.40.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Section XVII note 2(f): electrical machinery and equipment of chapter 85 is not a 'part of aircraft', which excludes heading 8807/8803", "GRI 1: heading 8516 covers electric heating resistors other than those of 8545", "GRI 6: 8516.80 electric heating resistors > 8516.80.40.00 assembled only with simple insulated former and electrical connections, used for anti-icing or deicing"], "rationale": "An electrical aircraft deicer is an electric heating resistor that warms surfaces to prevent ice formation. Section XVII note 2(f) excludes chapter 85 electrical equipment from aircraft parts, and heading 8516 names electric heating resistors. The HTSUS has a specific line, 8516.80.40.00, for heating resistors assembled only with a simple insulated former and electrical connections used for anti-icing or deicing. No precedent ruling on an aircraft deicer was found in the searches.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8807.30", "reason": "Section XVII note 2(f) excludes chapter 85 electrical goods from aircraft parts"}, {"code": "8516.80.80.00", "reason": "the deicer is used for anti-icing/deicing, which has its own line 8516.80.40"}, {"code": "8543.70.45.00", "reason": "covers defrosters and demisters with electric resistors for aircraft, but heading 8543 applies only to apparatus not specified elsewhere in chapter 85; a deicing heating resistor is specified in 8516.80.40 (would apply if the deicer is a complete apparatus with controls rather than a heating resistor)"}, {"code": "8533", "reason": "heading 8533 excludes heating resistors"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00091", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "various metalworking tools including metal slitting saws, taps, threading tools, drills, reamers, boring tools, and milling tools", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8211.93.0000):\nThe HTS US Code 8211.93.0000 is applicable for various metalworking tools as outlined in ruling number NY C83291. The classification includes specific tools such as metal slitting saws, taps, threading tools, drills, reamers, boring tools, and milling tools. Each tool is classified under different subheadings based on their specific characteristics and materials. \n\n1. **Metal Slitting Saws**: Classified under HTS 8202.31.0000, which covers circular saw blades with a working part of steel, with a duty rate of 0.6% ad valorem.\n   \n2. **Taps and Threading Tools**: Classified under HTS 8207.40.3000, which includes tools for tapping or threading with a cutting part containing specific percentages of chromium, molybdenum, tungsten, or vanadium, with a duty rate of 6% ad valorem.\n\n3. **Drilling Tools**: Classified under HTS 8207.50.2055, which pertains to tools for drilling (excluding rock drilling) with a cutting part containing specified percentages of chromium, molybdenum, tungsten, or vanadium, with a duty rate of 5.4% ad valorem.\n\n4. **Reamers and Boring Tools**: Classified under HTS 8207.60.0060, which covers tools for boring or broaching, with a duty rate of 5.3% ad valorem.\n\n5. **Cutters and End Mills (Milling Tools)**: Classified under HTS 8207.70.3030, which includes tools for milling with a cutting part containing specified percentages of chromium, molybdenum, tungsten, or vanadium, with a duty rate of 5.4% ad valorem.\n\nThe classification is supported by the detailed descriptions and material content requirements outlined in the ruling, confirming the accuracy of the HTS US Code for these tools.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8202.31.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Multiple different articles, not a GRI 3(b) set: classify the first-named article, metal slitting saws", "GRI 1: heading 8202 expressly covers blades for saws of all kinds, including slitting or slotting saw blades; this is more specific than milling tools of 8207.70", "GRI 6: 8202.3 circular saw blades (including slitting or slotting saw blades) > 8202.31.00.00 with working part of steel"], "rationale": "The description lists several different interchangeable metalworking tools that are not a retail set, so the first-named article, metal slitting saws, is classified. Heading 8202 expressly names slitting and slotting saw blades, and subheading 8202.31 covers circular saw blades with a working part of steel, giving 8202.31.00.00 on the assumption of HSS blades. The other tools fall in the heading 8207 subheadings for their kind. No precedent ruling specific to slitting saws was found in the searches.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8202.39.00.70", "reason": "applies if the working part is carbide or other non-steel material rather than steel"}, {"code": "8207.70", "reason": "milling tools; heading 8202 names slitting saw blades specifically"}, {"code": "8207.40 / 8207.50 / 8207.60", "reason": "taps and threading tools, drills and reamers are other articles to be classified separately"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00096", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "inductive loops and axle sensors used for traffic counting applications", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9029.90.8080):\nThe Autologger Loop and Autologger Axle Sensors are specifically designed for traffic counting and classification. The Autologger Loop operates by forming an inductive field that is disturbed by the passage of vehicles, allowing for vehicle counting. The Axle Sensors utilize piezo-electric or piezo-resistive technology to measure pressure changes caused by vehicles, enabling classification and speed measurement. Both products are categorized as parts and accessories of counters, which aligns with the description under HTS subheading 9029.90.8080. This classification is supported by the ruling that states these items fall under the category of parts and accessories for counting devices, and they are duty-free.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8543.70.45.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Several different articles named; classify the first-named: inductive loops", "Section XVI note 1(m) / chapter 90 considered: the loop is a sensing element that only transduces vehicle presence into an electrical signal; it does not itself count or measure, so heading 9029 (counters) and 9031 do not describe it", "GRI 1: heading 8543 covers electrical apparatus having individual functions n.e.s.i.; a transducer is named in subheading 8543.70.4x", "GRI 6: 8543.70 other machines > electric synchros and transducers... > 8543.70.45.00 other"], "rationale": "The description names two articles, so the first-named, inductive loops, is classified. An inductive loop embedded in the road changes inductance when a vehicle passes and sends that signal to a separate counter, so it functions as an electrical transducer rather than a counter or an insulated cable. Transducers are provided for in 8543.70.45.00 ('electric synchros and transducers ... other'), the line CBP used in N362482 for a magnetic axle sensor acting as a transducer. No ruling on traffic loops was found, so confidence is low.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8544.49", "reason": "the loop is a purpose-built sensing element, not merely an insulated conductor"}, {"code": "9029.90", "reason": "would require the loop to be a part of a counter; transducers are specified in chapter 85 (chapter 90 note 2(a) logic)"}, {"code": "8530.80", "reason": "traffic control/signalling equipment; the loops are used for counting, not controlling traffic"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00097", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a magnetic key cap that consists of a rubber cap and a neodymium-iron-boron magnet, allowing for the storage of keys by attaching to metal surfaces", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8505.11.0070):\nThe product in question is a magnetic key cap that includes a rubber component and a neodymium-iron-boron magnet. The key cap's primary function is to allow the user to store a key by attaching it to a metal surface using the magnet. According to the HTSUS, the applicable subheading for this product is 8505.11.0070, which covers permanent magnets and articles intended to become permanent magnets after magnetization, specifically those made of metal, such as sintered neodymium-iron-boron. The magnet is identified as the essential character of the product, which supports the classification under this HTS code. The ruling confirms that the classification is appropriate, and the duty rate for this item is 2.1 percent ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8505.11.00.70", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the components are prima facie classifiable in different headings: the rubber cap in 4016 and the magnet in 8505 (permanent magnets and articles of permanent magnet)", "GRI 3(b): the NdFeB magnet provides the article's function of attaching and holding keys, so it gives the essential character (GRI 3(c) would reach the same heading, 8505, as last in numerical order)", "GRI 6: 8505.1 permanent magnets > 8505.11 of metal > 8505.11.00.70 sintered neodymium-iron-boron (assumed sintered)"], "rationale": "The key cap is a composite good of a rubber cap and a neodymium-iron-boron permanent magnet. The magnet performs the article's function of attaching to metal surfaces to store keys, so under GRI 3(b) it controls, placing the article in 8505.11 as a metal permanent magnet and in statistical line .70 for sintered NdFeB. N333219 classified plastic articles backed with neodymium magnets in 8505.11.0070.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "4016.99", "reason": "the rubber cap is a housing; it does not give the essential character"}, {"code": "8505.19", "reason": "permanent magnets other than of metal (e.g., ferrite/bonded in plastic); NdFeB is a metal magnet"}, {"code": "8505.11.00.90", "reason": "applies only if the NdFeB magnet is bonded rather than sintered"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00104", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "the FW-300 NxStage Express Fluid Warmer and associated Warmer Disposable Sets", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8543.70.9650):\nThe FW-300 NxStage Express Fluid Warmer is classified under HTSUS 8543.70.9650 as it is an electrical machine designed to warm dialysate fluid prior to its administration in hemodialysis treatments. The device operates using electric heating resistors to warm the surface on which the fluid bag is placed, making it an apparatus that falls under the category of \"Other machines and apparatus\" in the electrical machines and apparatus section of the HTSUS. The Warmer Disposable Sets, which are specifically designed tubing assemblies for transporting dialysate fluid, are classified under HTSUS 9018.90.7570 as they are considered electro-medical instruments and accessories. However, the primary classification for the fluid warmer itself is 8543.70.9650 due to its function as an electrical apparatus rather than a medical instrument. Additionally, both items qualify for a secondary classification under HTSUS 9817.00.96 as they are specially designed for the benefit of individuals with end-stage renal failure, thus allowing for duty-free entry.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9018.90.75.70", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Section XVI note 1(m) and Section XVII-style exclusions not relevant; chapter 90 note 1 does not exclude it; heading 9018 covers instruments and appliances used in medical sciences, including electro-medical apparatus", "Chapter 90 note 2(b): the warmer and its disposable sets are not goods of a specific heading and are suitable solely or principally for use with NxStage dialysis apparatus, so they are classified with that apparatus", "GRI 6: 9018.90 other instruments > 9018.90.75 other (electro-medical) > 9018.90.75.70 parts and accessories of dialysis instruments and apparatus"], "rationale": "The FW-300 fluid warmer and its disposable sets heat dialysis fluid for use with the NxStage dialysis system, so they are medical appliances of heading 9018 rather than general electric heaters. Under chapter 90 note 2(b), accessories suitable solely or principally for dialysis apparatus are classified with it, giving 9018.90.75.70 (parts and accessories of dialysis instruments and apparatus). H81049 placed an electrically heated IV fluid warmer in 9018.90.75, and N351497 used 9018.90.7570 for hemodialysis console components.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9018.90.75.20", "reason": "the warmer does not itself perform dialysis; it is an accessory to the dialysis system"}, {"code": "9018.90.75.80", "reason": "residual line; the warmer is dedicated to dialysis apparatus, which has its own parts-and-accessories line"}, {"code": "8516.79 / 8543.70", "reason": "chapter 85 heaters are displaced by the more specific medical provision of 9018 for goods used in medical sciences"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00109", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "choker grapples and slack-pulling carriages used in the logging industry", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8431.49.1090):\nThe choker grapple and slack-pulling carriages are classified under HTS code 8431.49.1090 as they are considered parts suitable for use solely or principally with machinery of headings 8425 to 8430, specifically related to logging machinery. The choker grapple consists of two curved hinged metal jaws that are operated by a cable system to transport logs, while the slack-pulling carriages consist of several sheaves mounted in a housing that facilitate the horizontal movement of logs along the yarder line. In the ruling, it was determined that these items do not qualify as \"machinery, equipment, or implements\" under heading 9817.00.50 for agricultural purposes, as they are classified as parts rather than standalone machinery. Therefore, the correct classification for the choker grapple is under subheading 8431.49.1090, which pertains to parts of machinery used in the logging industry.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8431.39.00.70", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Several different articles; classify the first-named (choker grapples); the carriages follow the same analysis", "Section XVI note 2(b): the grapples and carriages are not goods of a specific chapter 84/85 heading and are suitable solely or principally for woodland log handling machinery (yarders/loaders) of heading 8428, so they go to heading 8431", "GRI 6: 8431.3 parts of machinery of 8428 > 8431.39.00 other > 8431.39.00.70 of the woodland log handling equipment of 8428.90.0310"], "rationale": "Choker grapples and slack-pulling carriages are dedicated working components of cable yarding and log handling equipment, which is lifting and handling machinery of heading 8428. Under Section XVI note 2(b) they fall in heading 8431 as parts of 8428 machinery, and the statistical line 8431.39.00.70 specifically covers parts of woodland log handling equipment of 8428.90.0310. H81340 classified a grapple hanger for log loaders as parts of 8428 machinery in 8431.39.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8431.39.00.80", "reason": "residual line; the goods are for woodland log handling equipment, which has its own statistical line .70"}, {"code": "8431.10", "reason": "parts of winches/hoists of 8425; yarding grapples and carriages serve log handling machinery of 8428"}, {"code": "8436.99", "reason": "forestry machinery parts of 8436 do not cover log handling/loading equipment of 8428"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00113", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "modified starches used in papermaking, specifically Cato Size 52A and Cato 15A", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 3505.10.0040):\nThe products Cato Size 52A and Cato 15A are classified under HTS Code 3505.10.0040, which pertains to \"Dextrins and other modified starches.\" The classification is based on the following reasoning:\n\n1. **Nature of the Products**: Both Cato Size 52A and Cato 15A are chemically modified starches, specifically cationic corn starches. They have been modified through etherification or esterification processes, which aligns with the definition of modified starches under HTS heading 3505.\n\n2. **Composition**: The primary component of both products is starch, with added ingredients (hydrocarbon defoamer for Cato Size 52A and phosphorous/silicon compounds for Cato 15A) constituting less than 1% of each product. This indicates that the starch is the predominant constituent, reinforcing their classification as modified starches rather than finishing agents.\n\n3. **Previous Classification**: The previous ruling (NY G81194) incorrectly classified these products under HTS subheading 3809.92.5000, which pertains to finishing agents used in the textile and paper industries. However, the analysis determined that the starch content and its modification characteristics are more appropriately classified under heading 3505.\n\n4. **Explanatory Notes**: The Explanatory Notes for heading 3505 clarify that it covers modified starches obtained through chemical processes. The inclusion of the products in this heading is supported by the World Customs Organization's classification opinions, which state that the added compounds do not alter the fundamental nature of the products as modified starches.\n\n5. **Final Determination**: Given that both products are specifically described as modified starches and that heading 3505 is the correct cl